\section{Theory}

Electric circuits are essentially combinations of electrical devices that
provide a pathway, potentially enabling charges to move. To understand how this
movement happens, we rely on a fundamental principle known as Ohm's law. Ohm's
law states that the loss of energy per charge, or potential difference $(V)$,
dictates how electricity behaves in a conductor, represented by the
formula:
\begin{equation}
    V = IR
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $V$ is the electric potential difference, or voltage (in volts, V)
    \item $I$ is the electric current (in amperes, A)
    \item $R$ is the electrical resistance (in ohms, $\Omega$)
\end{itemize}

To make voltage and current easily distinguishable, it helps to think of an
electrical circuit like a plumbing system. The electric current, represented by
$I$, is the actual physical flow of water moving through the pipes. In a circuit,
current is the flow of charged particles, like electrons. Mathematically, it is
defined as the rate of charge flow over time, given by the formula:
\begin{equation}
    I = \frac{q}{t}
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $I$ is the electric current (in amperes, A)
    \item $q$ is the amount of flowing charge (in coulombs, C)
    \item $t$ is the elapsed time (in seconds, s)
\end{itemize}

However, for water to flow through a pipe, it needs a push. In our analogy, that
push comes from a water pump, which acts just like a battery in an electric
circuit. The battery provides an electric potential difference, or voltage $(V)$.
Voltage is the energy per charge, measured in units of volts. Positively charged
particles naturally want to move from an area of higher electric potential to a
lower electric potential.

Finally, we have the pipes themselves. If a pipe is incredibly narrow, the water
will have a hard time getting through. In a circuit, this bottleneck is called
resistance $(R)$, measured in ohms $(\Omega)$. Resistors are simply materials that
decrease the flow of charges by dissipating the energy in the form of heat.

By understanding Ohm's law, we can fully justify our experimentation. Equation 2
$(V=IR)$ tells us that an electric potential difference $(V)$ will drive an
electric current $I$ given by $V/R$. Therefore, if we know the voltage pushing
the charges and the current flowing through, we can make a prediction of the
resistance. This lab tests whether these theoretical relationships hold up in
real, physical circuits when we arrange carbon resistors in different ways, such
as series or side-by-side in parallel.

\section{Conclusion}
\subsection*{Activity 1: Carbon Resistors}
On Activity 1, our team explored the properties of carbon resistors by first
identifying them using their standard color codes and then physically measuring
their resistance using an ohmmeter. We measured four resistors with values of
$326 \Omega$, $985 \Omega$, $10 020 \Omega$, and $21 600 \Omega$, which showed
an extremely low percent difference from the predicted color code
values—specifically $1.21 \%$, $1.50 \%$, $0.20 \%$, and $1.82 \%$ respectively.
Furthermore, when we mounted the resistors in series, we observed that their
total resistance accurately summed up to match our predictions,
yielding $1 330 \Omega$ for two resistors (only a $1.44 \%$ error) and an
impressive $33 000 \Omega$ for all four together (just a $0.21 \%$ error from
the expected  $32,931 \Omega$). When we mounted them in parallel, the combined
resistance appropriately dropped lower than the smallest individual resistor,
measuring $6 800 \Omega$ and $243 \Omega$ for two different pairs, closely
tracking the theoretical inverse addition predictions of $6 844.8 \Omega$
and $244.9 \Omega$.

\subsection*{Activity 2: Ohm's Law}
For Activity 2, we directly tested Ohm's law by placing our resistors in series
with a direct current (DC) power supply. We utilized a multimeter as a voltmeter
to measure the $6 V$ potential difference across the resistors and as an ammeter
to measure the electric current flowing through them. After analyzing the data,
our team measured voltage-to-current slope values of
approximately $2206$, $327.9$, $566.0$, and $1000$ $V/A$ for the individual
components. Furthermore, when testing the complete configuration, we measured an
overall system value of $897 \Omega$, which cleanly correlates to the calculated
theoretical resistance of the circuit $(896.8 \Omega)$. Overall, we found an
a consistent correlation between our experimental data and the theoretical
predictions, making it possible to safely validate Ohm's law and establish the
rules for how resistance adds up in both series and parallel configurations.

\section{Questions}

\subsection*{Activity 1 Questions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Compare the measured values of the four carbon resistors with
        their color code values and discuss the percent difference.} \\
        The four selected resistors had expected color-code values
        of $330~\Omega$, $1000~\Omega$, $10000~\Omega$, and $22000~\Omega$. When
        measured with an ohmmeter, the actual resistances
        were $326~\Omega$, $985~\Omega$, $10020~\Omega$, and $21600~\Omega$. The
        percent differences were exceedingly low b$1.21\%$, $1.50\%$, $0.20\%$,
        and $1.82\%$, respectively, demonstrating that the color-code bands
        provide a highly reliable estimation of a carbon resistor's true value.

  \item \textbf{Infer a simple rule for resistors in series. What is the
        expected value of the four resistors all mounted in series, and how does
        it compare to the actual measurement?} \\
        Based on the initial measurements, when resistors are mounted
        head-to-tail in series, their equivalent resistance is simply the sum of
        their individual resistances. Using this additive rule, the expected
        total resistance for all four components in series is $32931~\Omega$.
        The physical measurement yielded $33000~\Omega$, which is only
        a $0.21\%$ error, firmly supporting the series addition rule.

  \item \textbf{How is the new resistance of a parallel circuit related to the
        individual values? Predict the resistance of the first and second
        resistors mounted in parallel and compare with the actual measurement.}
        \\
        For resistors mounted in parallel, the inverse of the total equivalent
        resistance is equal to the sum of the inverses of each individual
        resistance. Using this relationship, the expected parallel resistance
        of $R_1$ ($326~\Omega$) and $R_2$ ($985~\Omega$) was calculated to
        be $244.9~\Omega$. The actual ohmmeter measurement was $243~\Omega$,
        confirming that parallel configurations decrease the overall resistance
        of the pathway.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Activity 2 Questions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{From your results, infer four general rules about voltages and
        currents in series and parallel configurations.} \\
        Based on the circuit behavior, the four established rules are: (1) In a
        series circuit, the electric current ($I$) remains constant across all
        components. (2) In a series circuit, the total voltage ($V$) is the sum
        of the individual voltage drops. (3) In a parallel circuit, the voltage
        ($V$) remains constant across all branches. (4) In a parallel circuit,
        the total electric current ($I$) is the sum of the currents flowing
        through each individual branch.

  \item \textbf{Using Ohm's law, measure the total resistance between points a
        and d. Explain your method.} \\
        To calculate the total resistance using Ohm's law ($R = V/I$), the total
        electric potential difference supplied by the DC battery ($6\text{ V}$)
        was divided by the total electric current entering the circuit at point
        ``a''. This mathematical approach treats the entire complex circuit as a
        single equivalent resistor.

  \item \textbf{Find the expected equivalent resistance between a and d using
        the established rules, and compare it with the multimeter measurement.}
        \\
        Using the series and parallel addition rules established in Activity 1,
        the theoretical equivalent resistance for the entire circuit was
        calculated to be $896.8~\Omega$. When the physical circuit was tested
        with a multimeter, the measured total resistance between ``a'' and ``d'' was
        exactly $897~\Omega$. This near-perfect alignment validates both Ohm's
        law and the theoretical rules for circuit analysis.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Above and Beyond}

While building simple circuits on a lab bench might seem like an abstract
exercise, the principles behind Ohm's law form the foundation of the modern
digital world. When we investigate how resistors limit current and drop
voltage, we aren't just looking at the dissipation of heat; at a deeper level we
are looking at a form of control.

Historically, early scientists and engineers realized that by carefully
arranging electrical components like resistors, they could dictate exactly how
electricity flows through a system. This ability to easily manipulate voltage
levels became a powerful way to represent information. A high voltage could
signify a $1$, and a low voltage could signify a $0$.

By arranging these electrical components into deliberate patterns, engineers and
scientists discovered how to construct physical logic gates. This was a
revolutionary breakthrough because it established a direct connection between
electrical behavior and Boolean algebra. It is incredibly profound to realize
that the exact same natural phenomenon that causes a tiny carbon resistor to
warm up during our lab experiment is the very mechanism that, when scaled into
microprocessors, allows us to calculate satellite trajectories, render
incredible graphics, and even process the text of this document. Nature provided
us with the predictable relationship between voltage, current, and resistance;
and the human ingenuity that harnessed that relationship to construct the computing
machines we take for granted today.

